# Slice 346 (the book :375, "внешний b получает результат"; colon-declares) and plan M11 5a (the author 2026-11-11):
# the holder of a merge result is declared by the receiver `@` -- `@: R merge(A; B)` declares the place R, and the
# receiver in its tail builds the Structure R holds; before plan M11 5v this was written `R: merge(A; B)`. Declared
# again by `@: R merge(B; A)`, R is one more occurrence: data\[0]R is the first result (its y), data\[1]R the second
# (its x); a bare R would be ambiguous. `R: A B` declares a Structure R with the atoms A and B (two fields). `S: S`
# declares S with the atom S inside, not expanded; declared again it is a second occurrence with one field -- no cycle.
# M11 5v: both merge declarations remain separate data occurrences. Plain
# retains the ordinary R without colliding with those two places. Root
# observations expose both complete copies after the data pass.
sub: main () (
    A:
        int: x 1
    B:
        int: y 2
    Plain:
        R: A B
    S: S
    S: S
    @: R merge(A; B)
    @: R merge(B; A)
) ()
main()
int: first main\data\[0]R\y
int: second main\data\[1]R\x
int: n length(main\data\Plain\R)
int: m length(main\data\[1]S)
@: observed_first main\data\[0]R
@: observed_second main\data\[1]R
